         <change when="2026-01-25T13:25:30-05:00" who="http://papyri.info/editor/users/ngonis">Finalized - done</change>
         <change when="2026-01-10T18:01:19-05:00" who="http://papyri.info/editor/users/ngonis">Vote - Accept-Straight-to-Finalization - Ed.'s μ(ητρὸς) is lin2 should be retained (mu + sinusoid + supralinear stroke). All other suggestions are fine. (Ed.'s τ̣ῷ̣ δ[ε]σ̣π̣ό̣(τῃ) [μου(?)] in lin1 is a fantasy, but the name appears to be irrecoverable.)</change>
         <change when="2026-01-10T17:06:20-05:00" who="http://papyri.info/editor/users/G.%20Papaioannou">Submit - L.1 λαμπροτάτῳ -&gt; λαμπρο̣(τάτῳ). The papyrus has λαμπρ with the usual oblique stroke and then what appears to be an omicron. Not that confident in omicron, it could also be part of the abbreviation sign. Cf. λαμπρότητος below.&#xD;
L. 2 Αὐρ(η)λίου -&gt; Αὐρ(ηλίου). After αυρ there is an oblique stroke going through ρ.&#xD;
μ(ητρὸς) -&gt;  μ(ητρὸ)ς. The papyrus has μc and a bar on top, like the nomen sacrum.&#xD;
L. 7 λαμπρότητος -&gt; λαμπρό̣(τητος). After λαμπρ an oblique stroke can be seen going through ρ and an o high on the line to the right (with a bar above it?).&#xD;
L. 12 Φλα(υίου) -&gt; Φλ(αυίου). λ is ligatured with the usual sinusoid used for abbreviations.</change>
